# Every layer a part reaches through the composition, itself included. One row per (layer, layer
# it reaches), and a layer always reaches itself.
#
# The third way of reaching, beside two the queries already name
#   `composition/descent.sqlc` is the layers reachable in one step or more.
#   `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc` is the layers that reach each other, which move together. This is
#   the layers reachable in one step or more, plus each part itself, and it is the one an inclusion
#   question needs: asking whether two parts reach a common layer has to count a part that is that
#   layer, because naming a layer directly while a sibling composes it is the commonest way for two
#   parts to overlap.
#
# Each part reaches itself, taken from the parts, not from the list of every layer
#   The rows where a layer reaches itself are the layers that appear as parts, taken from
#   `composition/parts.sqlc`. Taking them from `layers/every_layer.sqlc` would read the same on this
#   corpus and be a different relation: that list reaches everything, so composing it here would
#   put every filed layer among everything every reader composes, and `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc`
#   is the law that refuses exactly that.
#
# UNION, not UNION ALL
#   Reaching forgets how often. A layer reached by two routes is reached, and the question this
#   relation answers is whether, never how many times. Counting routes is `composition/descent`'s
#   job, where each route is still a row.
-- composition/descent.sqlc unioned with each part reaching itself, from composition/parts.sqlc.
SELECT DISTINCT root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
) w
  UNION
SELECT DISTINCT part_filing, part_layer, part_filing, part_layer
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) p
